#include(../Settings/defs.ves)

In the topological space, we consider the \lq\lq grade\rq\rq\ in the sense that
open sets can \lq\lq separate\rq\rq\ points well.

#def #T {T$_{#1}$}
#label(defin:separation-axioms)
useenv defin [Separation Axioms] {
    Let $X$ be a topological space. We say that $X$ is
    useenv enumerate [label=(\roman*)] {
        \item #T(0) space if $x,y\in X$ there exists an open set $U$
        such that either $x\in U$ and $y\notin U$, or $y\in U$ and $x\notin U$.
        \item #T(1) space if $x,y\in X$ there exist open sets $U\in\Nc_x$ and
        $V\in\Nc_y$ such that $y\notin U$ and $x\notin V$.
        \item #T(2) space if $x,y\in X$ there exist \it{disjoint} open
        sets $U\in\Nc_x$ and $V\in\Nc_y$.
        \item #T(3) space if $x\in X$ and a closed set $C$ in $X$ there
        exist \it{disjoint} open sets $U$ and $V$ such that $x\in U$ and
        $C\subset V$.
        \item #T(4) space if $C$ and $D$ are closed sets in $X$ there exist
        \it{disjoint} open sets $U$ and $V$ such that $C\subset U$ and $D\subset
        V$.
        \item #T(5) space if $C$ and $D$ are \it{separated} sets in $X$ there exist
        \it{disjoint} open sets $U$ and $V$ such that $C\subset U$ and $D\subset
        V$. Here, we say that two sets $C$ and $D$ are separated if $\cl{C}\cap D =
        C\cap \cl{D}= \emptyset$.\index{set!separated}
        \item #T(6) space if $C$ and $D$ are closed sets in $X$ there exists a
        continuous function $f:X->[0,1]$ such that $\{f=0\} = C$ and $\{f=1\} = D$.
    }
    \index{space!#T(0)} \index{space!#T(1)} \index{space!#T(2)} \index{space!#T(3)}
    \index{space!#T(4)} \index{space!#T(5)} \index{space!#T(6)} \index{space!Hausdorff}
    \index{space!regular} \index{space!normal} \index{space!completely normal}
    \index{space!perfectly normal}

    We also called
    #enum ((\alph*)) {
        \item T$_2$ space is also called by \it{Hausdorff space}.
        \item A \it{regular space} is both #T(1) and #T(3) spaces.
        \item A \it{normal space} is both #T(1) and #T(4) spaces.
        \item A \it{completely normal space} is both #T(1) and #T(5) spaces.
        \item A \it{perfectly normal space} is both #T(1) and #T(6) spaces.
    }
}

\subsection{#T(1) spaces}
First of all, we will study a proposition related to #T(1) spaces.

#label(prop:T1-singleton-closed)
useenv prop {
    Assume that $X$ contains at least two points.
    Then the space $X$ is #T(1) if and only if every singleton is closed.
    \proof
    Fix $x\in X$. Then for each $y\in X$ distinct with $x$, there exists an
    open set $U$ such that $x\notin U$ and $y\in U$.
    In other words, we find an open set $U$ satisfies $y\in U \subset X -! \{x\}$.
    As $y$ is an arbitrary points of $X -! \{x\}$, the set is open, hence $\{x\}$ is closed.
    
    Conversely, assume that every singleton is closed. Fix two distinct points
    $x$ and $y$ in $X$. Then take $U = X -! \{y\}$ and $V = X -! \{x\}$
    satisfies the property in the definition of #T(1) in \Cref{defin:separation-axioms}.
    The proposition is now proved. \proved
}

#label(exe:T0-singleton-closed)
useenv exec {
    From the proof of \Cref{prop:T1-singleton-closed}, one might think that every singleton
    should be closed also in #T(0) spaces. Prove or disprove that on #T(0) spaces,
    every singleton is closed.
}

\subsection{Hausdorff spaces}
Now we are going to study some properties of Hausdorff spaces.
First theorem gives an equivalent condition whether a space is Hausdorff.

useenv thm {
    The space $X$ is Hausdorff if and only if the diagonal set
    $$
        \Delta \defeq \{ (x,x) : x\in X\}
    $$
    is closed in the product space $X\times X$.
}

useenv exec {
    Let $X$ be a topological space and $Y$ be Hausdorff space.
    Let $f$ and $g$ are continuous function from $X$ into $Y$.
    Prove that the set
    $$
        \{f = g\} \defeq \{x \in X : f(x) = g(x)\}
    $$ 
    is closed in $X$. What if the condition \lq\lq Hausdorff\rq\rq\
    is dropped for $Y$?
}

Every Hausdorff space is indeed #T(1). Here is the proof.

#label(prop:hausdorff-T1)
useenv prop {
    Every Hausdorff space is #T(1).
    \proof
    Let $X$ be any Hausdorff space. There is nothing to prove if $X$ is
    singleton, thus we assume that $X$ contains at least two points.
    Fix $x_0\in X$. Then for any $y\in X$, we can take two disjoint open sets
    $U\in \Nc_{x_0}$ and $V\in\Nc_{y}$. In other words, we have $y\in V \subset
    X -! \{x_0\}$.
    Since $y$ is arbitrary element of $X-!\{x_0\}$, this proves that
    $X-!\{x_0\}$ is open, hence $\{x_0\}$ is closed.

    As $x_0$ is also choen arbitrary in $X$, this proves that $X$ is #T(1)
    because of \Cref{prop:T1-singleton-closed}. \proved
}

\subsection{Regular spaces and normal spaces}

#label(thm:urysohn-lemma)
useenv thm [Urysohn's Lemma] {
    Let $X$ be a normal space. If $C$ and $D$ are disjoint closed sets,
    there exists a continuous function $\phi:X->[0,1]$ such that $\phi \equiv 0$
    on $C$, and $\phi \equiv 1$ on $D$.
}

\subsection{Completely normal spaces}

useenv thm {
    The space $X$ is completely normal if and only if every subspace
    of $X$ is normal.
    \proof
    First, assume that $X$ is completely normal, fix any subspace $Y$ of
    $X$, and fix two disjoint closed subsets $A$ and $B$ of $Y$.
    Without loss of generality, assume that both $A$ and $B$ are not empty.
    Take two closed sets $E$ and $F$ in $X$ such that
    $$
        A = E \cap Y,\quad B = F\cap Y
    $$
    by \Cref{prop:subspace-topology-closed}. Since $A$ and $B$ are not empty, so
    are $E$ and $F$, respectively. Furthermore, as $A$ and $B$ are disjoint,
    $E$ and $F$ are also disjoint. From this fact and \Cref{exe:arith-closure},
    we have
    $$
        \overline{A} \cap B
        = \overline{E\cap Y} \cap (F\cap Y)
        \subset (\overline{E} \cap F) \cap (\overline{Y}\cap Y)
        = E \cap F \cap Y = \emptyset,
    $$
    showing that $\overline{A}\cap B$ is empty.
    Similarly, one can obtain that $A\cap\overline{B}$ is empty, thus $A$ and
    $B$ are separated. Since $X$ is completely normal, there exist disjoint open
    sets $U$ and $V$ such that $U \supset A$ and $V \supset B$.
    Then $U\cap Y$ and $V\\cap Y$ are open in $Y$, disjoint, and contains $A$
    and $B$, respectively. Therefore, we proved that $Y$ is normal because $A$
    and $B$ can be any disjoint closed sets.

    #TODO(Fill Here!)
}

useenv remark {
    If the space $X$ is just normal, there is no guarantee that
    a subspace is also a normal space.
    See \cite{counterexamples-topology} for the counterexample.
}

The example of completely normal space is an order space. One can see its proof
in \cite{counterexamples-topology}. Later (see
\Cref{remark:limit-measurability}) we use this fact to give some counterexample.

\subsection{Perfectly normal spaces}
Now we are going to study perfectly normal spaces. Like completely normal
spaces, there is an equivalent statement with the definition of perfectly normal
spaces.

#label(thm:vedenissof-thm)
useenv thm [Vedenissof's Theorem] {
    Let $X$ be a topological space. Then the following statements are
    equivalent.
    #enum ((\roman*)) {
        \item $X$ is perfectly normal.
        \item $X$ is #T(1) and for every open set $V$, there exists a continuous
        function $\phi:X->[0,1]$ such that $V = \{\phi > 0\}$.
        \item $X$ is normal, and every closed set is $G_\delta$, that is,
        every closed set is a countable intersection of open sets.
    }
    \proof
    If $X$ is perfectly normal, by definition, it is #T(1) space.
    Fix an open set $V$. If $V$ is empty, just take $\phi \equiv 0$.
    Otherwise, fix any point $x_0\in V$. By \Cref{prop:T1-singleton-closed},
    the set $\{x_0\}$ is closed. By the definition of #T(6) spaces, there exists
    a continuous function $\phi:X->[0,1]$ such that $\{\phi = 0\} = X-!V$, and
    $\{\phi = 1\}=\{x_0\}$.
    This, therefore, implies $V = \{\phi > 0\}$.
    
    On the other hand, if the converse assumption is satisfied, fix two disjoint
    closed sets $C$ and $D$. Take two continuous functions $\phi:X->[0,1]$ and $\psi:X->[0,1]$
    such that
    $$
        X -! C = \{\phi > 0\},\quad X -! D = \{\psi > 0\}.
    $$
    Since $C$ and $D$ are disjoint, we have
    $$
        C \subset X -! D = \{\psi > 0\},\quad
        D \subset X -! C = \{\phi > 0\}.
    $$
    Now define
    $$
        \theta(x) = {\phi(x) // \phi(x) + \psi(x)},\quad \forall x\in X.
    $$
    Then one can easily see that $0<=\theta<=1$, $\theta$ is continuous,
    $\{\theta=0\} = \{\phi=0\} = C$, and $\{\theta=1\} = \{\psi = 0\} = D$.
    Thus we proved that (i) and (ii) are equivalent.
    For that $\theta$, we can also prove that if $X$ is perfectly normal, then
    $X$ is normal. Indeed, consider $U = \{\theta < 1/2\}$, and $V = \{\theta >
    1/2\}$. Since $C = \{\theta=0\}$ and $D = \{\theta=1\}$,
    $C \subset U$, and $D\subset V$. Furthermore, it is clear that $U$ and $V$
    are disjoint, hence $X$ is normal as we can take arbitrary such $C$ and $D$.

    Now fix a closed set $F$ in $X$. From (ii),
    there exists a continuous
    function $\phi:X->[0,1]$ such that $X-!F = \{\phi > 0\}$, that is,
    $$
        F = \{\phi = 0\} = \bigcap_{n=1}^oo ?\{ \phi < {1//n} \}?.
    $$
    Now notice that $\{\phi<1/n\}$ is open in $X$ for every $n=1,2,3,...$,
    because $\phi$ is continuous.
    
    On the other hand, assume that (iii) holds. Since $X$ is normal, we can
    apply \Cref{thm:urysohn-lemma}. Fix any closed set $F$ in $X$. Since $F$ is
    $G_\delta$, one can take a sequence of open sets $(V_n)_n$ such that $F =
    \bigcap_{n=1}^oo V_n$. Then for each $n$, use \Cref{thm:urysohn-lemma} to
    take a continuous function $\phi_n:X->[0,1]$ such that $\phi_n \equiv 0$ on
    $F$, and $\phi_n\equiv 1$ on $X-!V_n$. Then the function
    $$
        \phi = \sum_{n=1}^oo {\phi_n // 2^n}
    $$
    is the desired one to make $F = \{\phi = 0\}$.
    Therefore, we proved that (ii) and (iii) are equivalent. The proof is now
    finished. \proved
}

useenv remark {
    \Cref{thm:urysohn-lemma} implies that for any disjoint closed sets $C$ and $D$,
    there exists a continuous function $\phi:X->[0,1]$ such that $C \subset \{\phi = 0\}$,
    and $D\subset \{\phi = 1\}$. From this perspective, perfect normality is much
    stronger than normality.
}

To end this section, we will prove relations between several separation axioms.
